      "text": "Here are the confirmed publication details for **To Kill a Mockingbird** by Harper Lee, perfect for your syllabus:\n\n---\n\n### 📚 Publication Details\n\n| Detail | Information |\n|---|---|\n| **Author** | Harper Lee |\n| **Original Publication Year** | **1960** |\n| **Original Publisher** | **J.B. Lippincott & Co.** |\n| **ISBN (current standard edition)** | 978-0-06-112008-4 |\n\n---\n\n### 📖 Notable Edition History\n\n- **1960 – First Edition** (J.B. Lippincott & Co.): The original hardcover publication. The novel became an immediate bestseller and won the **Pulitzer Prize for Fiction in 1961**.\n- **1960s onward – Paperback editions**: The book was widely issued in paperback, making it accessible for classroom use and helping cement its place in American curricula.\n- **HarperCollins editions**: After J.B. Lippincott was acquired, HarperCollins became the primary publisher of the novel and remains so today. Current trade paperback and hardcover editions are published under HarperCollins/Perennial Modern Classics.\n- **2002 – Perennial Modern Classics edition**: A widely adopted classroom edition, often cited in academic syllabi.\n- **2010 – 50th Anniversary Edition**: Released to commemorate the novel's golden anniversary, featuring special introductory material.\n- **2015 – *Go Set a Watchman* (companion novel)**: Harper Lee published this novel, which is considered an early draft/predecessor to *To Kill a Mockingbird* rather than a true sequel, also through HarperCollins. Its release sparked renewed scholarly interest in the original novel.\n\n---\n\n### ✏️ Syllabus Tip\nFor citation purposes, you'll most likely want to reference the **HarperCollins Perennial Modern Classics** edition (ISBN: 978-0-06-112008-4) as it is the most widely available and commonly used in academic settings. The **2010 50th Anniversary Edition** is also a great option if you want to highlight the novel's enduring significance in your course framing.",
