ce & the Failure of the Legal System** — The trial exposes how systemic racism can corrupt even formally impartial institutions like the courts.\n- **Moral Courage** — Atticus embodies the idea that true courage means doing what is right in the face of community pressure and certain defeat.\n- **Empathy & the Limits of Judgment** — Atticus's famous instruction to Scout — to \"climb into someone's skin and walk around in it\" — frames the novel's persistent interrogation of prejudice, whether racial, social, or personal.\n- **Childhood, Innocence & Disillusionment** — The novel traces the painful process by which children absorb and reckon with adult moral failures.\n- **Social Class & Southern Hierarchy** — Maycomb's rigid caste system shapes nearly every relationship and conflict in the novel, situating race within a broader structure of social stratification.\n- **The \"Mockingbird\" Motif** — The title's central symbol — that it is a sin to kill a mockingbird because it does no harm — applies to both Tom Robinson and Boo Radley as figures of innocence destroyed or threatened by a cruel society.\n\n---\n\n### ✏️ Suggested Course Description Language\n> *Harper Lee's Pulitzer Prize–winning novel (1960) uses the retrospective childhood narrator Scout Finch to probe the moral failures of the Depression-era American South. Through the wrongful prosecution of Tom Robinson and the redemptive arc of Boo Radley, the novel engages enduring questions of racial injustice, empathy, moral courage, and the loss of innocence — making it a foundational text in the American literary and civic tradition.*\n\n---\n\nThis should give you everything you need for your registrar description and course framing. Let me know if you'd like suggested discussion questions, pairing texts, or further scholarly context!",
